WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:10.280
A compromised container can become a node risk when the workload can interact with the shared kernel, host filesystem, device interfaces, runtime, or node services beyond its intended scope.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.900
The node is therefore part of container security, not merely infrastructure underneath it.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.960
The shared kernel processes system requests from every container on the node.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:26.540
Kernel maintenance, supported versions, security updates, and reduction of exposed functionality are important control assumptions.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.680
Workload restrictions reduce exposure but do not remove the kernel dependency.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.500
Host filesystem access can expose configuration, logs, application data, or credentials.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:43.520
Node metadata and credentials may allow the node to authenticate to registries, orchestrators, cloud services, or storage.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:50.540
Protect these assets with least privilege, short-lived credentials where possible, and careful metadata access controls.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.080
Kubelet and container-runtime interfaces perform powerful management operations.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:03.140
Restrict network and local access, authenticate and authorize administration, protect configuration, and monitor use.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.640
Ordinary workloads should not inherit node-management reachability.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:19.180
Node design can reduce blast radius through hardened images, minimal services, dedicated workload pools, controlled administration, secure boot and integrity features where supported, and separation of workloads with different trust or sensitivity.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.440
Multi-tenant assumptions deserve explicit review.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.020
Response planning should address both workload and node.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:36.560
Teams need criteria for isolating or draining a node, preserving evidence, replacing it from a known-good image, rotating affected credentials, and safely restoring workloads.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.300
Automation helps only when these actions and their availability impact are understood in advance.
